    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you provide a comparative analysis between signalized intersections and modern roundabout designs in terms of vehicle throughput and delay mitigation? I'm specifically looking at how these two configurations handle peak-hour congestion and the concept of \"flow friction\" in high-density urban environments.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Signalized intersections typically offer higher peak-hour throughput at cost of significant delay due to strict queuing and flow control, creating rigid bottlenecks in dense urban areas. In contrast, modern roundabouts utilize an all-way yield design to maintain continuous traffic flow, effectively reducing \"flow friction\" by minimizing 90-degree turning conflicts and eliminating the stops inherent in signal cycles. While roundabouts generally excel at mitigating delay and improving capacity during congestion through smoother vehicle movement, they often require larger signalization dead zones or are best suited for lower-speed intersections, whereas signals remain superior for separating conflicting traffic streams in extremely high-density corridors. Ultimately, the optimal choice depends on whether the priority is maximum theoretical capacity via signals or more robust congestion resilience and driver efficiency via roundabouts.",
      "source": "target",
