    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you expand a bit more on the \"flow friction\" aspect? I'm particularly interested in those unpredictable disruptions—like sudden braking or unexpected lane changes—that seem to occur even when the geometry of the intersection is technically sound. How would you quantify or model those types of non-linear interruptions in a simulation?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "\"Flow friction\" in simulations is often modeled through non-linear Decision-Making Units (DMUs) within cellular automata or macroscopic traffic models, where agents react dynamically to local density rather than just fixed geometry. These disruptions are quantified by introducing stochastic acceleration and stopping functions that decrease approaching speeds unpredictably, effectively creating phantom queues that block downstream space even when theoretical capacity is high. By assigning random reaction times and braking sensitivity to each virtual vehicle, the model captures the cascading \"phantom stop-and-go\" waves that degrade actual throughput below theoretical limits.",
      "source": "target",
